Not quite.  I would accept that but it really would have to be *very* 
bad luck.  I don't like gambling, and I don't think, you should get into 
a situation that 30 hours of building up a character depend on gambling. 
  I personally would leave the game then.  One of its great assets to me 
is that I know it can be beaten despite the RNG, once you have overcome 
the completely random aspects of the initial game.  So far, this 
conviction has proved to be true: none of my characters having an AC 
better than -10 has really died to bad luck.  In *all* cases but two it 
was stupidity on my part and in the remaining cases lack of knowledge 
(of course you can call it bad luck, if a water demon summons Yeenoghu 
on Dlvl 1, but it was not bad luck that I attacked this water demon in 
the first place).

As I read it, Martin threw the game away out of frustration, not because 
it was already unwinnable.  He #quit, he did not die.  I don't think you 
can call that a loss to bad luck.

